<h2>What the lists tell you</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Walnut is the #1 finish.</strong> Walnut Natural leads the finish list, Walnut Bison is #8, and walnut is the #2 species. If your showroom has no walnut door on the wall, that's the first gap to close.</li>
  <li><strong>White and off-white paint is still the volume.</strong> Maple Polar, Pure White, Maple Beige, and Maple Shoji White hold four of the top five finish spots — and maple is the #1 species, because it's the painted-cabinet substrate.</li>
  <li><strong>Rift White Oak is the wood-tone story.</strong> Three of the top ten finishes are on Rift White Oak — Flagstone, Straw, and Yosemite Brown — and Rift Cut White Oak is the #3 species. Show at least one light and one mid-tone rift oak. (See how 5 Blox put <a href="/playbook/5blox-shiloh-eclipse-yosemite-brown-display/">Yosemite Brown on display</a>, and our <a href="/playbook/grain-matched-white-oak-metro-straw/">Straw walkthrough</a>.)</li>
  <li><strong>Rustic and character species still sell.</strong> Hickory (#4) and Rustic Alder (#5) both outrank plain White Oak. In mountain and ranch markets, don't let a modern-leaning sample wall push those clients elsewhere.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Turning the data into a sample-wall plan</h2>

<p>A practical rule: if a finish or species is in the top ten and you can't put a door in a client's hands, you are relying on a chip to close the sale. Audit your door samples against both lists, and fill the gaps in rank order. Newer finishes released since February — like <a href="/playbook/paint-look-white-oak-stains/">the heavy-pigment White Oak stains</a> and <a href="/playbook/maple-honey-stain/">Maple Honey</a> — are worth adding on top, because they're what designers will ask about next.</p>

